        <help_tag>More information</help_tag>
        <help_text>What is the consequence of the worker's exposure to the Hazard?&#13;
1. Minor - Injuries that could require first aid treatment. Worker returns to work within 1 day. Normally reversible. Examples include minor scrapes, bruises, or small lacerations.&#13;
2. Moderate - Injuries that require medical attention with return to work generally within 3 days. Normally reversible. Examples include deep flesh wounds, sprains, or minor bone fractures&#13;
3. Serious - Injuries that require medical attention with return to work likely after 3 days. Normally reversible. Examples include major bone fractures or second degree burns.&#13;
4. Major - Injuries that require medical attnetion with worker suffering long-term reduction in quality of life. Normally not reversible. Examples include compound fractures, musculoskeletal disorders resulting in permanent disability, blindness, loss of limbs or third degree burns.&#13;
5. Fatal - Injuries that result in immediate death or subsequent death. Examples include inhalation of poisonous gasses that have an immediate or long-term effect on the central nervous system</help_text>
